canvasplayer
------------

Graffiti Markup Language (GML) playback in the browser, on HTML5 <canvas>,
with no dependencies.

Online demo: https://jamiew.github.io/canvasplayer
Code: https://github.com/jamiew/canvasplayer

GML data comes from #000000book, an open database of GML files:
https://000000book.com/data/147.json
https://000000book.com/data/147.gml
https://000000book.com/data/147


Running it locally
------------------

No build step, so any static server works:

    python3 -m http.server 8420

Open http://localhost:8420/?id=161. Also ?latest and ?random. Tags come from
000000book.com over JSONP, so you need the network but not a server.

To debug a tag, turn on the DATA switches: "points" numbers the strokes and
marks every sample, "vectors" draws per-sample velocity, "bounds" shows the
capture screen against the drawing, "graph" plots speed. A GML file is usually
messier than it looks.

    node gml-player.test.js
    node gml-source.test.js


Using it
--------

    <div class="stage"><canvas id="canvas"></canvas></div>
    <script src="gml-player.js"></script>
    <script src="gml-source.js"></script>
    <script>
      GmlSource.load(147, function (tag) {
        new GmlPlayer(document.getElementById('canvas'), tag).play();
      });
    </script>

The canvas sizes itself to its parent, so give that element the dimensions you
want. gml-player.js has no dependencies and does not care where its data came
from -- feed it the same shape from anywhere:

    { id, app, rotate, strokes: [ {points: [[x, y, time], ...]} ] }

gml-source.js is only the #000000book adapter: it fetches a tag and flattens
the GML tree into that. It tries fetch() and falls back to JSONP, because the
API sends no CORS headers yet.


What it does
------------

Plays a tag in real time from the recorded timestamps, repairing the dead time
between strokes and saying so. Width follows the speed of the hand and tapers
like a marker. Ink pools where the pen dwelled, and runs from there.

Eight modes. marker, smooth and chisel are handstyles: marker follows the
recorded speed, smooth runs a spline through the samples to take the staircase
off a slow hand, and chisel sweeps a flat nib at a fixed angle, so width comes
from direction rather than speed. hairline, outline, dots, spray and skeleton
are the diagrammatic ones.

Five effects: ghost, pressure, bleed, jitter, fade. pressure drops the alpha
where the hand moved fast, bleed lays a wider faint pass under the stroke.

It opens with the samples, velocity vectors, speed graph and ghost turned on,
which is the view that shows what the file actually holds. The bounding box is
the one overlay left off.

Playback is monochrome in both light and dark, so the overlays separate
themselves by weight, dashes and alpha rather than colour.


What changed in v4
------------------

Dropped Processing.js, and fixed the maths it inherited:

* playback advanced one point per frame, so a dense tag fell behind its own
  timeline. That is what the downsampling hack worked around
* stroke weight came from `sqrt(pow(dimx,2), pow(dimy,2))`. sqrt takes one
  argument, so width only ever tracked horizontal speed
* landscape captures were rotated by `rotate(80)`, which Processing reads as
  80 radians
* the canvas was a fixed 800x580, so taller tags were cropped


What changed in v5
------------------

One frame, one size. The frame used to take the shape of each drawing, so
switching tags resized the box and moved everything under it. It is a fixed
square now, with the drawing fitted inside.

Orientation comes from <environment><up>. Tags before about #170 have none, and
the old code guessed from the client's name. That was wrong: Graffiti Analysis
1.0 wrote the tag's name there, not the app's, so #161 is "katsu-4", matched
/Katsu/, and played on its side. #818 and #1399 are "hell" and "seen".

The geometry answers it instead. Both axes are normalized against the same edge
of the capture screen, so an upright drawing cannot leave 0..1. The env-less
DustTag captures all reach y = 1.3..1.5, the 480px edge in units of the 320px
one.

That also means the axes are already square. v4 reapplied the screen's 3:2
ratio on top, which squashed every landscape capture.

Parser: <drawing> can repeat (#100 has five) and <tag> can arrive as an array.
#100 used to come back with no strokes at all.

Light and dark themes, following the system until you pick one.

Dropped the glow effect, and the offscreen compositing that existed to serve
it.


